<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <key>Label</key>
    <string>com.alphalete.incident-triage</string>

    <!-- Sorts the OPEN incidents in #claudecorrections-and-requests into
         "needs a person" (red circle), "Lucy is retrying it" (pending) and
         "waiting on a source" (purple), so the channel list answers "which of
         these is mine?" without opening anything.

         SAFE: the only things it writes are reactions on incident posts and one
         line in a thread when an incident changes state. It never re-runs a
         report, never edits code, never posts anywhere else. A bad fire costs
         one wrong emoji.

         NOTE for anyone editing this comment: no double hyphen is allowed
         inside an XML comment. plutil accepts one and launchd runs the job, but
         Python's plistlib refuses the whole file, which drops the job out of
         schedule_guard and schedule_audit and spawns a phantom Hub card. That
         cost six plists on 2026-08-26. Write flag names without their dashes.

         Install: lucy rerun install_incident_triage_agent -->

    <!-- Path is the laptop placeholder; install_agent.py rewrites it to the
         target machine's repo path. -->
    <key>ProgramArguments</key>
    <array>
        <string>/bin/bash</string>
        <string>/Users/megan/1st Claude Folder/deploy/incident_triage.sh</string>
    </array>

    <key>WorkingDirectory</key>
    <string>/Users/megan/1st Claude Folder</string>

    <key>EnvironmentVariables</key>
    <dict>
        <key>PYTHONPATH</key>
        <string>/Users/megan/1st Claude Folder</string>
    </dict>

    <!-- Machine local, all 7 days. Seven days because the flow runs seven days.

         08:15 is the FIRST pass, not the only one: after the 4am flow's main
         passes and the 7:30 checkpoint email, and before the noon backstop, so
         it never grades a report the orchestrator is still actively retrying.

         WHY IT REPEATS (Megan 2026-08-27, on an incident that alerted at 07:28
         and wore no circle all day). One pass a day had two holes:

           1. classify() flips everything still open to "needs one of you" once
              the clock passes BACKSTOP_HOUR = 12, because past the backstop the
              retry loop has stopped trying. Firing only at 08:15 made that rule
              unreachable: nothing ever ran with now_hour >= 12, so an incident
              graded "Lucy has this" at breakfast still said so at 5pm.
           2. a mark that fails to land, or an incident that opens AFTER 08:15,
              had no second pass to fix it. The channel simply carried no answer
              until the next morning.

         Every 2 hours through the working day closes both: the noon rule gets
         real fires, and a missed mark self heals inside two hours. Repeating is
         cheap and quiet by design, because the reaction is idempotent and the
         thread line is posted once per STATE CHANGE, not once per pass. -->
    <key>StartCalendarInterval</key>
    <array>
        <dict><key>Hour</key><integer>8</integer><key>Minute</key><integer>15</integer></dict>
        <dict><key>Hour</key><integer>10</integer><key>Minute</key><integer>15</integer></dict>
        <dict><key>Hour</key><integer>12</integer><key>Minute</key><integer>15</integer></dict>
        <dict><key>Hour</key><integer>14</integer><key>Minute</key><integer>15</integer></dict>
        <dict><key>Hour</key><integer>16</integer><key>Minute</key><integer>15</integer></dict>
    </array>

    <key>StandardOutPath</key>
    <string>/Users/megan/1st Claude Folder/output/logs/incident-triage.launchd.out.log</string>
    <key>StandardErrorPath</key>
    <string>/Users/megan/1st Claude Folder/output/logs/incident-triage.launchd.err.log</string>
</dict>
</plist>
